 here. &#x60;bulk&#x60; is the high-volume engine and is metered HERE, on the source characters of the strings it translated: a fully-cached rebuild, or a string that failed, costs zero. BULK NEVER FALLS BACK TO QUALITY — on a deployment that does not serve it the answer is 503 for that tier, so a caller is never quietly served, or charged, at a tier it did not ask for. A bulk request beyond its balance is refused with the nested {\&quot;error\&quot;:{\&quot;code\&quot;,\&quot;message\&quot;}} body at 402/503.  &#x60;model&#x60; names the model the quality tier runs on, through the same metered model plane as the default (enso-auto), which decides whether your org may use it exactly as it does for chat. That is how an org points translation at a model it serves itself, on its own linked GPU say: by the alias it registered for it. A name from an upstream model family is rewritten to the default before it runs, so a model of your own must be named by your org&#39;s alias; the reply&#39;s &#x60;model&#x60; names the one it runs on. &#x60;model&#x60; with &#x60;tier: bulk&#x60; is 400.  &#x60;target&#x60; IS CHECKED FOR SHAPE, NOT FOR SUPPORT: anything BCP-47-shaped is accepted (&#x60;es&#x60;, &#x60;pt-BR&#x60;), anything else is 400. There is no unsupported-language error — a well-formed tag no engine can actually render is passed straight through, and whatever comes back is what gets stored and returned. &#x60;format&#x60; (text, html, markdown) tells the engine what markup to preserve; &#x60;glossary&#x60; fixes terms verbatim.  A BATCH IS TRANSLATED IN PARTS of about 8 KB, each its own completion with a ceiling sized to it. A part that comes back cut short (any finish other than stop), malformed, or covering the wrong number of strings is asked again one string at a time. Every translation must keep its source&#39;s shape: the same {placeholders}, printf conversions in the same order, the same ICU arguments and selectors with an other branch (the words in their branches are translated), the same tags with the same attributes (alt, title, aria-label and placeholder are words), the same link targets and URLs; a string whose shape moved is asked once more. A string that still fails comes back with &#x60;error&#x60; naming what went wrong (a lost placeholder, an added URL) and no text, is not stored, and fails nothing else.  THE WORK IS BOUNDED: at most two model calls per part, one held for every part still to come, a stop after two parts in a row return nothing, 90 seconds per call, and a stop when the caller disconnects. Strings the call did not reach come back with &#x60;error&#x60; saying why. What came back is stored, so sending the batch again asks only for the rest. When no string at all can be returned, or the engine fails, the answer is 502 with code &#x60;translate_failed&#x60;.  Requires a validated principal — 401 without one, and the org is always that principal&#39;s. At most 512 strings per call and 32768 characters per string (400 past either), and 262144 characters in all (413 past it: split the batch).
